        "thinking": "So many server routes import values from `@paperclipai/db`. These are all table references for drizzle queries. When esbuild bundles these, it includes all of `@paperclipai/db`'s exports including the embedded-postgres stuff.\n\nThe correct fix here is NOT to try to change all these imports. Instead, I need to either:\n\n1. **Separate the db package**: Put the schema exports in a subpath that doesn't pull in embedded-postgres\n2. **Mark embedded-postgres as external**: Tell esbuild not to try to bundle it (this is the right approach for CF workers)\n\n\n3. **Use package.json exports conditions**: Set up a `worker` or `browser` condition in `@paperclipai/db/package.json` that excludes embedded-postgres\n\nOption 3 or 1 would be cleanest long-term, but marking embedded-postgres as external in the build is the fastest path forward. Wrangler supports a `[build]` configuration section where I can specify external dependencies, so I can leverage that instead of needing a custom build command.\n\nActually, the real issue is that `@paperclipai/db` is exporting Node-only utilities like `getEmbeddedPostgresTestSupport` and `createEmbeddedPostgresLogBuffer` from its main entry point, which shouldn't be consumed by the CF worker at all. The proper fix is either moving these to a separate entrypoint like `@paperclipai/db/embedded` or using export conditions in package.json, though I need to verify this won't break other parts of the codebase first. Looking at what's actually exported from the db package, I need to trace through each file to see which ones pull in embedded-postgres dependencies. The test utilities and error handling functions definitely do, but I should verify whether the core database client functions in `client.ts` also depend on it, and check what `runtime-config.ts` is importing since that's also exposed in the main exports.",
        "type": "thinking"
      }
